<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">World models, which simulate environmental dynamics and generate sensor observations, are gaining increasing attention in autonomous driving.
However, progress in LiDAR-based world models has lagged behind those built on camera videos or occupancy data, primarily due to two core challenges: the inherent disorder of LiDAR point clouds and the difficulty of distinguishing dynamic objects from static structures.
To address these issues, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GEM</span>: a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>enerative LiDAR world model that leverages d<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>formable <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>amba architecture, significantly improving fidelity and imaginative capability.
Specifically, leveraging the structural similarity between sequential laser scanning and Mamba’s processing mechanism, we first tokenize LiDAR sweeps into compact representations via a custom LiDAR scene tokenizer.
After unsupervised disentanglement of tokenized features via a dynamic-static separator, a tri-path deformable Mamba is introduced to perform selective scanning and adaptive gating fusion over the disentangled features, leading to enhanced spatial-temporal understanding of the world evolution.
Optionally, a planner and a BEV layout controller can be integrated to explore the model’s capability for autonomous rollout and its potential to generate “what-if” scenarios.
Extensive experiments show that GEM achieves state-of-the-art performances across diverse benchmarks and evaluation settings, demonstrating its superiority and effectiveness. Project page: <a href="https://github.com/wuyang98/GEM" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/wuyang98/GEM</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07326v1/introduction.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:328/181;" width="328" height="181" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) Previous methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> fail to adequately model LiDAR-specific properties, suffer from entangled dynamic-static features, and must rely on ground truth ego status, limiting their ability to understand the driving world.
(b) In contrast, our approach explicitly disentangles dynamic and static elements following LiDAR’s physical structure.
This enables more accurate scene comprehension while supporting autonomous rollout and reliable “what-if” reasoning.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">World models represent a transformative technology, enabling autonomous vehicles to evolve from passively reacting to their environment to actively reasoning about the future <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
While significant progress has been made for camera video-based and occupancy-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, the potential of LiDAR-based world models remains largely unexplored, despite LiDAR’s inherent advantage in providing precise geometric capture of driving environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Developing a robust LiDAR world model faces two primary challenges.
The first is the unstructured nature of point clouds, which prevents the direct application of well-developed techniques for structured data like camera video and occupancy.
This fundamental mismatch compels existing methods to efficiently convert point clouds into intermediate representations.
For instance, some methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> project LiDAR data into dense voxel or Bird’s-Eye-View (BEV) features. However, these conversions sacrifice fine-grained geometric detail, consequently degrading prediction fidelity.
Other approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> instead map point clouds into range images to leverage conventional feature extractors.
A key limitation of these approaches is their reliance on convolution- or transformer-based architectures, which remain inherently misaligned with the sequential scanning mechanism of laser sweeps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second challenge stems from the inherent ambiguity in raw point clouds, which lack discriminative cues like texture from camera videos or semantic labels from occupancy data.
This absence hinders LiDAR world models from effectively comprehending the evolving patterns of dynamic objects from the static environment.
As shown in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), previous methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> fail to provide explicit scene decomposition, resulting in reduced geometric accuracy, noticeable temporal inconsistencies, and limited environmental comprehension.
Furthermore, these approaches lack autonomous rollout and controllable generation capabilities, which are critical for scalable world modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a LiDAR world model named <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GEM</span> to address the aforementioned challenges.
As illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (b), GEM is built on three key innovations:
(1) a compact representation dedicated to LiDAR scanning;
(2) the explicit disentanglement of dynamic and static elements;
and (3) optional support for autonomous rollout (autonomously planning future ego status and forecasting future observations) and controllable generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach is grounded in the structural similarity between sequential laser scanning and the processing mechanism of Mamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Inspired by this, we first introduce a LiDAR scene tokenizer on top of the Mamba architecture, which excels at preserving the inherent physical structure of LiDAR data for robust sequential modeling.
Building upon this representation, we explicitly disentangle the dynamic and static scene components to enhance the model’s comprehension of world evolution.
Specifically, we devise an unsupervised dynamic-static separator to separate dynamic and static features without costly annotations.
The disentangled features are then processed by a tri-path deformable Mamba that captures both local object evolution and global scene context in parallel.
Finally, an adaptive gated attention dynamically evaluates the importance of dynamic and static features and fuses them into a coherent scene representation.
On nuScenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and KITTI Odometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, GEM achieves new state-of-the-art performance for 1s and 3s predictions.
Beyond the core world modeling, GEM can integrate autonomous rollout and external guidance,
enabling controllable “what-if” scenario reasoning for driving world.
Contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a LiDAR scene tokenizer based on the Mamba architecture, designed for compact and structurally faithful latent representation of LiDAR data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a LiDAR world model with explicit dynamic-static disentanglement in driving scenes, comprising three core components:
an unsupervised feature separator that isolates dynamic-static elements without manual labels,
a tri-path deformable Mamba that models global context while preserving feature separation,
and an adaptive gated fusion mechanism for robust, context-aware aggregation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework makes a pioneering contribution to LiDAR world models by introducing optional autonomous rollout and controllable generation capabilities which are previously unattainable by previous methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments on various benchmarks demonstrate that GEM achieves state-of-the-art performance, validating the effectiveness of its design.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">World Models in Autonomous Driving.</span>
Given historical observations, a world model aims to predict future states.
It is gaining growing attention in autonomous driving for its capability to generate high fidelity data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> and enhance driving safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
In autonomous driving, three mainstream world models have emerged: vision-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, occupancy-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and LiDAR-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
While former two types of world models are widely explored, the LiDAR counterpart remain less studied despite their capability to provide precise geometric comprehension and greater scalability.
Among these, several works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> utilize sequence modeling techniques to predict future states.
4D-Occ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> employs voxel as an intermediate representation, and Copilot4D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> enhances model performance through compressed BEV feature interaction.
While existing methods overlook the properties of LiDAR data, we addresses this gap through meticulous designs, achieving not only more accurate prediction but also autonomous rollout and controllable generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LiDAR Data Generation.</span>
Acquiring large-scale LiDAR data is time-consuming and costly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, spurring growing research into LiDAR generation as a promising alternative.
Earlier methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> construct virtual scenes or physical models for generation, but they suffer from limited diversity and fidelity due to reliance on costly assets and hand-tuned parameters.
Recently, learning-based generative methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> directly model the distribution of LiDAR data, enabling large-scale synthesis and controllable sampling.
While existing methods can produce high-fidelity single frame data, they fail to maintain temporal consistency across sequential scans.
In contrast, our method can generate LiDAR sequences that are not only visually realistic but also temporally coherent.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07326v1/vqgan.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:672/142;" width="672" height="142" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Our LiDAR scene tokenizer first projects the point cloud <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> into a dense range map <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A scan-based Mamba encoder then process the map into latent features <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are discretized into <math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> via a vector quantization codebook.
After this, a decoder is used to perform the upsampling and reconstruct <math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> into output <math id="S2.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is finally converted back into a point cloud <math id="S2.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐏</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To enhance the reconstruction quality, particularly for range maps dominated by low-frequency signals, a discriminator is incorporated into our training process.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LiDAR Representation and Learning.</span>
Selecting an appropriate representation is crucial for LiDAR data generation.
While raw point clouds provide an intuitive data format, the irregular nature forces methods like PointNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> to devote approximately 90% of runtime to data organization rather than meaningful computation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, rendering it impractical for large-scale LiDAR generation.
As an alternative, voxel representation structures point clouds into volumetric grids, enabling feature extraction through CNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>.
However, this approach suffers from quantization error that compromise generation quality.
In contrast, range map representation offers a balanced solution.
Derived directly from LiDAR’s polar output, this 2.5D format preserves geometric precision while maintain structural regularity.
Although compatible with CNN, we argue that the range map’s inherent structure aligns more naturally with Mamba’s sequential scanning, enabling more effective feature learning for LiDAR generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section first describes the problem formulation for our LiDAR world model in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
We then detail our dedicated tokenizer for LiDAR data compression in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 LiDAR Scene Tokenizer ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, and finally present our LiDAR world model with dynamic-static disentanglement in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Deformable Mamba for LiDAR World Model ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">World models, which predict future sensor data based on historical observations, are becoming an increasingly prominent technique in fields like autonomous driving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and embodied intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
Formally, at a given timestamp <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, a world model takes <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> historical observations <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{p}=\{\mathbf{P}^{u},\mathbf{P}^{u-1},\cdots,\mathbf{P}^{u-\tau_{p}+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{p}=\{\mathbf{P}^{u},\mathbf{P}^{u-1},\cdots,\mathbf{P}^{u-\tau_{p}+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and their corresponding ego status <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> as input, with the primary objective of predicting <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> future frames <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{f}=\{\mathbf{P}^{u+1},\mathbf{P}^{u+2},\cdots,\mathbf{P}^{u+\tau_{f}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{f}=\{\mathbf{P}^{u+1},\mathbf{P}^{u+2},\cdots,\mathbf{P}^{u+\tau_{f}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note two key variations in previous methods.
First, future ego status <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> may be provided as ground-truth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, predicted by a separate planner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, or generated by the world model itself <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.
Second, some models can accept additional control signals (<em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, BEV layouts) to enable controllable generation, a feature of significant practical value for scenario-based testing.
Inspired by recent latent diffusion-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> that successfully integrate these versatile functions, we similarly build our GEM framework following a latent diffusion paradigm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Pipeline.</span>
The foundation of our framework is the LiDAR scene tokenizer described in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 LiDAR Scene Tokenizer ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, which serves two key purposes: it maps unordered point clouds into organized feature sequences and significantly reduces the computational cost of the subsequent diffusion process.
This module consists of an encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> that compresses point clouds into compact latent features and a decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> that reconstructs LiDAR data from these features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We first tokenize the historical point clouds <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain latent features <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}^{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau_{p}\times h\times w\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}^{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau_{p}\times h\times w\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following the approach of COME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, control signals, including historical and future ego status along with optional BEV layouts if provided, are encoded into control features.
All features are processed by our Mamba-based world model detailed in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Deformable Mamba for LiDAR World Model ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, producing an output <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\hat{Z}}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau_{f}\times h\times w\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\hat{Z}}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau_{f}\times h\times w\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then decoded by <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> into the final predicted point clouds.
Next, we details our LiDAR scene tokenizer and Mamba-based world model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07326v1/flowchart.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:672/267;" width="672" height="267" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
The process of feature disentanglement and aggregation in GEM.
First, dynamic-static separator separates the latent features in an unsupervised manner into three components: dynamic features <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, static features <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and generic features <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The tri-path deformable Mamba then adaptively generates specialized scan paths <math id="S3.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p}_{g},\mathbf{p}_{d},\mathbf{p}_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p}_{g},\mathbf{p}_{d},\mathbf{p}_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each branches, which guide the sampling of intermediate features <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{g},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{d},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{g},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{d},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> via bilinear interpolation.
These features are then passed by Mamba within their respective branch, obtaining <math id="S3.F3.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathcal{F}_{g}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{d}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{s}^{{}^{\prime}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{F}_{g}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{d}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{s}^{{}^{\prime}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, an adaptive gated attention fuses these features to produce the refined feature <math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for future frame generation.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>LiDAR Scene Tokenizer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The unordered and sparse nature of point clouds presents a significant challenge for effective feature extraction.
We identify Mamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> as a highly suitable architecture for this task, as its core mechanism of feature aggregation through sequential scanning closely mirrors the physical process of LiDAR laser sweeps.
Building on this observation, we propose the LiDAR scene tokenizer for compact data representation, as illustrated in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2 Related Works ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Specifically, we first convert the input LiDAR point cloud <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> into a range map <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> via spherical projection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the number of vertical lasers and the horizontal resolution per scan, respectively.
Each element in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> stores the distance for a specific laser at a specific azimuth.
The <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is then processed by a Mamba-based encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of the initial Mamba scan operation followed by six blocks of common layers, as shown in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2 Related Works ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
As our implementation uses the standard Mamba operation, we omit its internal details and direct the readers to the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> or our Appendix for more information.
With the spatial down-sampling occurs in the last block of the block, we can get the latent code <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During reconstruction, the latent representation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is first quantized via a codebook to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This quantized latent is then passed through a decoder <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, shown in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2 Related Works ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, to reconstruct the range map <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{R}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{R}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A vector-quantized reconstruction loss is utilized for model training:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{VQ}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}||\mathcal{R}-\hat{\mathcal{R}}||+\beta\cdot||\mathtt{sg}(\mathbf{z})-\hat{\mathbf{z}}||+||\mathbf{z}-\mathtt{sg}(\hat{\mathbf{z}})||," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝚅𝚀</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{VQ}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}||\mathcal{R}-\hat{\mathcal{R}}||+\beta\cdot||\mathtt{sg}(\mathbf{z})-\hat{\mathbf{z}}||+||\mathbf{z}-\mathtt{sg}(\hat{\mathbf{z}})||,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a balancing hyperparameter and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{sg}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{sg}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stop-gradient operation during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{VQ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝚅𝚀</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{VQ}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the mean-square error tends to produce overly smooth predictions for nearby pixels.
However, the accurate reconstruction of such high-frequency information is critical for range maps, as it corresponds to essential geometric features like object edges and scene structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>.
To address this spectral bias and enhance reconstruction fidelity, we introduce a discriminator <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> with an extra adversarial training objective, which is illustrated in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2 Related Works ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{ADV}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}[\mathtt{log}(\mathcal{S}(\mathcal{R}))+\mathtt{log}(1-\mathcal{S}(\hat{\mathcal{R}}))],\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝙰𝙳𝚅</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚕𝚘𝚐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝚕𝚘𝚐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{ADV}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{R}}[\mathtt{log}(\mathcal{S}(\mathcal{R}))+\mathtt{log}(1-\mathcal{S}(\hat{\mathcal{R}}))],\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and the whole loss for our LiDAR scene tokenizer is:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{LST}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{VQ}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{ADV}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝙻𝚂𝚃</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝚅𝚀</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝙰𝙳𝚅</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{LST}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{VQ}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{ADV}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Deformable Mamba for LiDAR World Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section first details our Mamba-based feature extractor, which comprises the dynamic-static separator and tri-path deformable Mamba as shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
We then outline the process for generating future frames from enhanced features and conclude with an overview of controllable generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic-Static Separator.</span>
Compared to camera videos and occupancy data, LiDAR data provides relatively weaker semantic cues for scene understanding, which hinders world models from efficiently comprehending environmental evolution.
This limitation motivates us to explicitly separate dynamic objects from the static environment to enhance the model’s representational capacity.
To avoid labor-intensive semantic labeling and improve scalability, we propose to accomplish this disentanglement in an unsupervised manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Leveraging the observation that dynamic objects exhibit variation across frames while static environments remain largely consistent, we derive two complementary cues: frame difference and temporal averaging.
For brevity, the denoising timestep <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is omitted in the feature expressions below.
Given a latent feature <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau\times h\times w\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau\times h\times w\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the dynamic pattern <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> via frame-wise differences:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{d}[i]=\begin{cases}\mathbf{0},&amp;\text{if }i=1,\\
\mathcal{Z}[:,i]-\mathcal{Z}[:,i-1],&amp;\text{if }i=2,3,\cdots,\tau.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒵</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟎</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒵</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒵</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{d}[i]=\begin{cases}\mathbf{0},&amp;\text{if }i=1,\\
\mathcal{Z}[:,i]-\mathcal{Z}[:,i-1],&amp;\text{if }i=2,3,\cdots,\tau.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the static pattern, we apply a temporal average within a sliding window of size <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{Z}_{s}[i]=\frac{1}{\mathtt{end}-\mathtt{start}}\sum\nolimits^{\mathtt{end}-1}_{i=\mathtt{start}}\mathcal{Z}[i],\ i=1,2,\cdots,\tau,\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒵</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝚎𝚗𝚍</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝚜𝚝𝚊𝚛𝚝</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝚎𝚗𝚍</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝚜𝚝𝚊𝚛𝚝</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒵</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="1.087em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{Z}_{s}[i]=\frac{1}{\mathtt{end}-\mathtt{start}}\sum\nolimits^{\mathtt{end}-1}_{i=\mathtt{start}}\mathcal{Z}[i],\ i=1,2,\cdots,\tau,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{start}=\mathtt{max}(0,i-\lfloor n/2\rfloor),\mathtt{end}=\mathtt{min}(\tau,i+\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚜𝚝𝚊𝚛𝚝</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚖𝚊𝚡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝚎𝚗𝚍</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚖𝚒𝚗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{start}=\mathtt{max}(0,i-\lfloor n/2\rfloor),\mathtt{end}=\mathtt{min}(\tau,i+\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1)</annotation></semantics></math> define the window bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we employ three parallel extractors with identical 3D convolutional structures to transform <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z},\mathcal{Z}_{d},\mathcal{Z}_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z},\mathcal{Z}_{d},\mathcal{Z}_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> into features <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F},\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F},\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To effectively fuse these features, we introduce an adaptive gated attention inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, which performs data-dependent fusion:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}}&amp;=(1-\mathtt{G}(\mathtt{C}[\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}]))\cdot\mathcal{F}_{d}+\mathtt{G}(\mathtt{C}[\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}])\cdot\mathcal{F}_{s},\\
\mathcal{F}_{g}&amp;=(1-\mathtt{G}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}))\cdot\mathcal{F}+\mathtt{G}(\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}})\cdot\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝙶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝙶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>𝙶</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝙶</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}}&amp;=(1-\mathtt{G}(\mathtt{C}[\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}]))\cdot\mathcal{F}_{d}+\mathtt{G}(\mathtt{C}[\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}])\cdot\mathcal{F}_{s},\\
\mathcal{F}_{g}&amp;=(1-\mathtt{G}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}))\cdot\mathcal{F}+\mathtt{G}(\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}})\cdot\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{G}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{G}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a gating function composed of convolutional layers and activations, and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{C}[\cdot,\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{C}[\cdot,\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes channel-wise concatenation.
Ultimately, we have <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for dynamic and static cues, along with a fused generic feature <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07326v1/dynamic_static.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:328/136;" width="328" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of scan path deformation on range maps and LiDAR point clouds. Warmer colors in the heatmap indicate a higher concentration of path points shifted to those regions. We visualize the displacement trends of the top-15 most frequently deformed path points.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tri-path Deformable Mamba.</span>
After obtaining dynamic and static cues, we propose a tri-path architecture to leverage them for disentangled region modeling while maintaining global coherence.
We begin with a brief overview of deformable Mamba scanning and interpolated feature generation for later clarity.
Given an input generic feature <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau\times h\times w\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau\times h\times w\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> together with customized scan paths <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}\in\mathbb{R}^{(\tau\times h\times w)\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}\in\mathbb{R}^{(\tau\times h\times w)\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\{g,d,s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\{g,d,s\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the generic, dynamic, and static paths, the deformable Mamba mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> aims to aggregate features along this path and refines them into the output feature <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{\gamma}=\mathtt{BI}(\mathcal{F}_{g},\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}),\quad\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}}_{\gamma}=\mathtt{DM}(\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{\gamma}),\quad\gamma\in\{g,d,s\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝙱𝙸</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>γ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝙳𝙼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{\gamma}=\mathtt{BI}(\mathcal{F}_{g},\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}),\quad\mathcal{F}^{{}^{\prime}}_{\gamma}=\mathtt{DM}(\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{\gamma}),\quad\gamma\in\{g,d,s\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{BI}(\mathcal{F}_{g},\mathbf{p}_{\gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙱𝙸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{BI}(\mathcal{F}_{g},\mathbf{p}_{\gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes bilinear interpolation for sampling <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> along path <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{DM}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙳𝙼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{DM}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes performing Mamba operation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> at the deformed feature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, as illustrated in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, each branch in our tri-path deformable Mamba performs the two-step procedure defined in <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.3 Deformable Mamba for LiDAR World Model ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
The first step involves feature aggregation via a branch-specific reference path <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the generic branch, we directly use the standard path <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{(\tau\times h\times w)\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{(\tau\times h\times w)\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from the 3D coordinate space <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{g}=\mathtt{Flatten}(\mathtt{Meshgrid}(\mathtt{Linspace}(\mathbb{C})))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝙵𝚕𝚊𝚝𝚝𝚎𝚗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙼𝚎𝚜𝚑𝚐𝚛𝚒𝚍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙻𝚒𝚗𝚜𝚙𝚊𝚌𝚎</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{g}=\mathtt{Flatten}(\mathtt{Meshgrid}(\mathtt{Linspace}(\mathbb{C}))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">For the dynamic and static branches, however, the model need to learn to focus on the corresponding elements.
A key challenge is customizing these paths effectively.
We address this by leveraging the pre-computed features <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}_{d},\mathcal{F}_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> as indicators for the locations of dynamic and static elements.
These features are processed through fully connected layers to generate specialized paths:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbf{p}_{d}=\mathbf{p}_{g}+\mathtt{Tanh}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathtt{ReLu}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathcal{F}_{d})))),\\
\mathbf{p}_{s}=\mathbf{p}_{g}+\mathtt{Tanh}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathtt{ReLu}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathcal{F}_{s})))).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚊𝚗𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙻𝚒𝚗𝚎𝚊𝚛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝙻𝚞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙻𝚒𝚗𝚎𝚊𝚛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚊𝚗𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙻𝚒𝚗𝚎𝚊𝚛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝙻𝚞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝙻𝚒𝚗𝚎𝚊𝚛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbf{p}_{d}=\mathbf{p}_{g}+\mathtt{Tanh}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathtt{ReLu}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathcal{F}_{d})))),\\
\mathbf{p}_{s}=\mathbf{p}_{g}+\mathtt{Tanh}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathtt{ReLu}(\mathtt{Linear}(\mathcal{F}_{s})))).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Guided by these learned paths, the scanner can direct its attention toward either dynamic objects or the static background, effectively separating the respective information, which is verified by our visualizations in <a href="#S3.F4" title="In 3.3 Deformable Mamba for LiDAR World Model ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Using these three distinct paths, we obtain interpolated features <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{g},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{d},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{g},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{d},\mathcal{\bar{F}}_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with each deforming to different regions of the driving scene.
Subsequently, the three features are processed by Mamba within their respective branches, obtaining <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathcal{F}_{g}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{d}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{s}^{’}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>’</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{F}_{g}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{d}^{{}^{\prime}},\mathcal{F}_{s}^{’}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with each capturing different aspects of the driving scene.
The outputs from these three branches are then fused via our adaptive gated attention mechanism to produce an updated generic feature <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, completing one processing block of tri-path deformable Mamba.
In our implementation, we stack four such blocks, which iteratively refine <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for the final generation task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latent Diffusion Paradigm.</span>
We employ the diffusion paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> for generation.
During training, the target future point clouds are encoded into latent features <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau_{f}\times h\times w\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tau_{f}\times h\times w\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Over <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> diffusion steps, noise is progressively added to <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> at each step <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to form a noisy latent <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}^{f}_{t}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\mathcal{Z}^{f}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>f</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}^{f}_{t}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\mathcal{Z}^{f}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a noise schedule weight that decreases with <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is random noise.
In the denoising stage, the corresponding refined <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> at each timestamp <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is processed by a convolutional layer to get the predicted noise <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\epsilon}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\epsilon}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The training objective of our latent diffusion process is to minimize the error between predicted and actual noise, formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{LD}}(\bm{\epsilon}_{t},\hat{\bm{\epsilon}}_{t})=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{1})}[||\bm{\epsilon}_{t}-\mathtt{WM}(\mathcal{Z}^{f}_{t},\mathcal{Z}^{p}_{t},\mathbf{t},\mathbf{c};\bm{\theta}_{\mathtt{WM}})||^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝙻𝙳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝚆𝙼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>𝚆𝙼</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{LD}}(\bm{\epsilon}_{t},\hat{\bm{\epsilon}}_{t})=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{1})}[||\bm{\epsilon}_{t}-\mathtt{WM}(\mathcal{Z}^{f}_{t},\mathcal{Z}^{p}_{t},\mathbf{t},\mathbf{c};\bm{\theta}_{\mathtt{WM}})||^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{\mathtt{WM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>𝚆𝙼</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{\mathtt{WM}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learnable parameters of the world model, and <span id="S3.SS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">t</span> is the timestamp embedding.
<span id="S3.SS3.p8.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">c</span> is the optional control signals introduced below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">After training, the model synthesizes future latents through an iterative denoising process.
This begins with an initial noise latent <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\hat{Z}}^{f}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\hat{Z}}^{f}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and progressively recover it over <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps to produce the final prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Controllable Generation.</span>
In real-world applications, future ego status <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> are typically unknown in advance, which is a critical issue overlooked by previous methods.
To address this, we incorporate an optional planner to predict these status. Inspired by BEV-Planner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, our planner is jointly learned with world model to fully leverage perceptual and ego status information as:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{Planner}}(\mathbf{a}^{f},\hat{\mathbf{a}}^{f})=||\mathbf{a}^{f}-\mathtt{Planner}(\mathbf{a}^{p};\bm{\eta}|\bm{\theta}_{\mathtt{WM}})||^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝙿𝚕𝚊𝚗𝚗𝚎𝚛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝙿𝚕𝚊𝚗𝚗𝚎𝚛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜼</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>𝚆𝙼</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{Planner}}(\mathbf{a}^{f},\hat{\mathbf{a}}^{f})=||\mathbf{a}^{f}-\mathtt{Planner}(\mathbf{a}^{p};\bm{\eta}|\bm{\theta}_{\mathtt{WM}})||^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground truth ego status, <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{a}}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{a}}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted ego status, and <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the parameters of the planner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Following established practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, we encode timestamps and ego status, along with optional signals like BEV layouts, into feature embeddings. These are fused via adaptive group normalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> to form the conditioning vector <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.3 Deformable Mamba for LiDAR World Model ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ GEM: Generating LiDAR World Model via Deformable Mamba" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
This design offers significant flexibility through a unified architecture that accommodates various control signals types and sources.
The complete training objective for our world model is:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{LD}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{Planner}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝙻𝙳</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝙿𝚕𝚊𝚗𝚗𝚎𝚛</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{LD}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathtt{Planner}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07326v1/visual_nuscenes_kitti.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:642/214;" width="642" height="214" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visual comparisons with competitive LiDAR world models on nuScenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and KITTI Odometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> datasets.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:298%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:30%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:30%;">Quantitative comparisons of world modeling accuracy with other state-of-the-art methods on nuScenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.10" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:237.5pt;height:77.1pt;vertical-align:-36.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-350.8pt,113.8pt) scale(0.252890363148972,0.252890363148972) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.10.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.10.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">Methods</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">Horizon</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">CD</span><sub id="S4.T1.10.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:298%;">inner</span></sub><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;"> </span><math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="2.980em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">L1</span><math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{sr}\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="2.980em">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="2.980em">r</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="2.980em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{sr}\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">AbsRel</span><math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{sr}\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="2.980em">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="2.980em">r</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="2.980em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{sr}\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">L1 </span><math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="2.980em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">AbsRel </span><math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="2.980em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.1.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">CD </span><math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="2.980em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.10.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">SPFNet</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">1s</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">2.24</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">-</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">-</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">4.58</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">34.87</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">4.17</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.10.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">3s</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">2.50</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">-</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">-</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.3.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">5.11</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.3.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">32.74</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.3.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">4.14</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.10.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">S2Net</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.10.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.25pt;padding-bottom:2.25pt;"><span id="S4.T1.10.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:298%;">1s</span></td>
